{"title":"2003 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 23","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Note\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Note</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(Meta-Solving_Technique)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (Meta-Solving Technique)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A large <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Equilateral_triangle\" title=\"Equilateral triangle\">equilateral triangle</a> is constructed by using toothpicks to create rows of small equilateral triangles. For example, in the figure, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> rows of small congruent equilateral triangles, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> small triangles in the base row.  How many toothpicks would be needed to construct a large equilateral triangle if the base row of the triangle consists of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/1/b910f4dafca80b54c6e7bedf63b6485eebff0764.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2003$\" width=\"35\" height=\"12\" /> small equilateral triangles? \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/c/16ca1f0305d713b2b9e5a0c53658d11829856ec7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(15mm); defaultpen(linewidth(.8pt)+fontsize(8pt)); pair Ap=(0,0), Bp=(1,0), Cp=(2,0), Dp=(3,0), Gp=dir(60); pair Fp=shift(Gp)*Bp, Ep=shift(Gp)*Cp; pair Hp=shift(Gp)*Gp, Ip=shift(Gp)*Fp; pair Jp=shift(Gp)*Hp; pair[] points={Ap,Bp,Cp,Dp,Ep,Fp,Gp,Hp,Ip,Jp}; draw(Ap--Dp--Jp--cycle); draw(Gp--Bp--Ip--Hp--Cp--Ep--cycle); for(pair p : points) { fill(circle(p, 0.07),white); } pair[] Cn=new pair[5]; Cn[0]=centroid(Ap,Bp,Gp); Cn[1]=centroid(Gp,Bp,Fp); Cn[2]=centroid(Bp,Fp,Cp); Cn[3]=centroid(Cp,Fp,Ep); Cn[4]=centroid(Cp,Ep,Dp); label(&quot;$1$&quot;,Cn[0]); label(&quot;$2$&quot;,Cn[1]); label(&quot;$3$&quot;,Cn[2]); label(&quot;$4$&quot;,Cn[3]); label(&quot;$5$&quot;,Cn[4]); for (pair p : Cn) { draw(circle(p,0.1)); }[/asy]\" width=\"225\" height=\"195\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/3/0837bddd8e8fdd5cecc04a04167021c986d2d342.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(A) \\ } 1,004,004 \\qquad \\mathrm{(B) \\ } 1,005,006 \\qquad \\mathrm{(C) \\ } 1,507,509 \\qquad \\mathrm{(D) \\ } 3,015,018 \\qquad \\mathrm{(E) \\ } 6,021,018$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"695\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/3/f13ba9f93ef56169929553418383922dafb68bb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+3+5+...+2003=1002^{2}=1004004$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"323\" height=\"16\" /> small equilateral triangles. \n</p><p>Each small equilateral triangle needs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> toothpicks to make it. \n</p><p>But, each toothpick that isn't one of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/d/24df3828d1ba088836fae8c9eba1968ac5a58ef4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1002\\cdot3=3006$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"118\" height=\"13\" /> toothpicks on the outside of the large equilateral triangle is a side for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> small equilateral triangles. \n</p><p>So, the number of toothpicks on the inside of the large equilateral triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/1/831b7b5676a0a2b1842f5b3670141a30f675328e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1004004\\cdot3-3006}{2}=1504503$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"238\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore the total number of toothpicks is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/c/d0c98d4d0572c24886c62b7f469b22b619a542ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1504503+3006=\\boxed{\\mathrm{(C)}\\ 1,507,509}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"270\" height=\"29\" />. \n~dolphin7\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We just need to count upward facing triangles because if we exclude the downward-facing triangles, we won't be overcounting any toothpicks. The first row of triangles has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> upward-facing triangle, the second row has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> upward-facing triangles, the third row has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> upward-facing triangles, and so on having <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> upward-facing triangles in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/5/3657b37b69a5e1029f9182897c6b5dc7492cc1bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^\\text{th}$\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" /> row. The last row with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/1/b910f4dafca80b54c6e7bedf63b6485eebff0764.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2003$\" width=\"35\" height=\"12\" /> small triangles has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/a/63a8720f439e8e8887a0746430412c323e81e13c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1002$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> upward-facing triangles. By <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Triangular_number\" title=\"Triangular number\">Gauss's formula</a>, the number of the upward-facing triangles in the entire triangle are now <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/7/fe7c4e0f55a5db118407be24b20fdcfc42a2b73c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1002\\times1003}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"97\" height=\"37\" />, meaning that the number of toothpicks are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/a/ada473f8b019d281093f37afa617b1aedbebcf13.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1002\\times1003}{2}\\times3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"132\" height=\"38\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/e/2fe478b2a8093bf254c3c9322e8e9d932d5af6bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\mathrm{(C)}\\ 1,507,509}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"124\" height=\"29\" />.\n</p><p>~mathpro12345\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Note\">Note</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Note\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>You don't have to calculate the value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/a/ada473f8b019d281093f37afa617b1aedbebcf13.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1002\\times1003}{2}\\times3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"132\" height=\"38\" />, and you can use units digits to find the answer easily. The units digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/6/8a6554a8e06e479445d02be2e279a19724f06955.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1002\\times1003$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"94\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and has a unit digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> after being divided by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Then this is multiplied by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, now the final number ending with a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. This leaves only one answer choice possible, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/e/2fe478b2a8093bf254c3c9322e8e9d932d5af6bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\mathrm{(C)}\\ 1,507,509}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"124\" height=\"29\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Test out some smaller cases first.\n</p><p>When there is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> equilateral triangle in the base, you need <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> toothpicks.\nWhen there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> equilateral triangles in the base, you need <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> toothpicks in all.\nWhen there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> equilateral triangles in the base, you need <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/7/5e7a1b6098a8c98b8e4adae526aeef4b91712620.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$18$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> toothpicks in all.\nWhen there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> equilateral triangles in the base, you need <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/1/651ba418a810d971dbc8a326d49a71fe1541fae0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> toothpicks in all.\n</p><p>Taking the finite differences, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/a/25a8e7a658123a76226358a4a1c6343ab33d3f8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6, 9, 12.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" /> It forms a linear sequence. This means the original numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/1/cb1813c2fec77f4517e463e6d86a50d9be8f64b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3, 9, 18, 30)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"91\" height=\"18\" /> form a quadratic.\n</p><p>Let the quadratic be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/8/878740b1e9bad0008122348eca48d8883a00e7ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = ax^2 + bx + c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"132\" height=\"18\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/8/08861c774a31d25c0d044952c00df7850efe057e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = 2 \\cdot \\text{(number of equilateral triangles in base)} - 1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"387\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Then, we have the following points: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/c/3bc288a276f8bb20c1cc49075b8fdf681d9f1ce8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1, 3), (2, 9), (3, 18), (4, 30).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"207\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>We can plug these values into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/8/878740b1e9bad0008122348eca48d8883a00e7ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = ax^2 + bx + c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"132\" height=\"18\" />, giving: \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/d/68d63eda80865471ce09ec436a36cf325a35b1e5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a + b + c = 3, 4a + b + c = 9, 9a + 3b + c = 18.\\]\" width=\"364\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Solving gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/d/7fd292131d0718f0700bac0d29566affb59f2b62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = b = 1.5, c = 0.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"142\" height=\"16\" /> So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/7/3c7e95ae7c302c00ea33805957ed6c489e08014d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y = 1.5x^2 + 1.5x.\\]\" width=\"133\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>For our problem, we need <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/1/b910f4dafca80b54c6e7bedf63b6485eebff0764.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2003$\" width=\"35\" height=\"12\" /> equilateral triangles in the base. For the quadratic, the corresponding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-value would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/2/2d21693d283af9cd06864280ff1793b3ace6c4ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2003 + 1}{2} = 1002$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"134\" height=\"38\" />. So our answer is simply: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/2/f027793ad41e7590370e578ad6a8f0c47752a6b6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1.5 \\cdot 1002^2 + 1.5 \\cdot 1002 = \\boxed{\\mathrm{(C)}\\ 1,507,509}.\\]\" width=\"325\" height=\"30\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can count the slanted toothpicks and the horizontal toothpicks separately. Since the bottom row has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/1/b910f4dafca80b54c6e7bedf63b6485eebff0764.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2003$\" width=\"35\" height=\"12\" /> triangles, there will be toothpicks between the triangles and on the edges, which makes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/a/90ae760d1ea10eb7a90d22087b3c7e2393d02117.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2004$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"36\" height=\"13\" /> toothpicks on the bottom row. The row above it will have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> fewer triangles, so it will have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> fewer toothpicks. This continues all the way to the top. Therefore, the number of slanted toothpicks is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/2/0b240ef1a21286e720a4d7d240b988342aa6e0b4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2004 + 2002 + 2000 + \\cdots + 4 + 2 = 1002^2 + 1002.\\]\" width=\"388\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>The horizontal toothpicks are easier. For each row, we will only count the toothpicks on the bottom of the row to avoid overcounting. Since the bottom row has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/1/b910f4dafca80b54c6e7bedf63b6485eebff0764.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2003$\" width=\"35\" height=\"12\" /> toothpicks, there will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/a/63a8720f439e8e8887a0746430412c323e81e13c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1002$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> toothpicks below the row and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/8/888c508062c09735bab7ff0e11e7dc80cc7580db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1001$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> toothpicks above the row (which is the bottom of the next row). This continues all the way to the top, so the number of horizontal toothpicks is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/d/b7d89572babdb43a3abdf51cc2b72c49e14ccff5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1002 + 1001 + 1000 + \\cdots + 2 + 1 = \\frac{(1002)(1003)}{2}.\\]\" width=\"391\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Adding these together, we get our answer of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/5/115beb1958d432c952769cab0a164e7ef369ffdc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1002^2 + 1002 + \\frac{(1002)(1003)}{2} = \\boxed{\\mathrm{(C)}\\ 1,507,509}.\\]\" width=\"380\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>~jk11507\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28Meta-Solving_Technique.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(Meta-Solving_Technique)\">Video Solution (Meta-Solving Technique)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (Meta-Solving Technique)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/GmUWIXXf_uk?t=494\">https://youtu.be/GmUWIXXf_uk?t=494</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10A\" title=\"2003 AMC 10A\">2003 AMC 10A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2003 AMC 10A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2003 AMC 10A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2003 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 22\">Problem 22</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2003 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 24\">Problem 24</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=